## Why This Is Asked

Tests concrete application of 3-valued logic to pipeline gating, not just theory. Requires constructing a satisfiable partial assignment and a propagation strategy that avoids prematurely forcing F.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic: T>U>F ordering; ¬, ∧, ∨, → semantics
- Backtracking/forward-chaining under partial info
- Satisfiability with Unknowns in real-world policy constraints

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple Kleene-like 3-valued evaluator for our constraints
const V = {T:2, U:1, F:0};
function not(x){ return x===V.T?V.F : x===V.F?V.T : V.U; }
function or(a,b){ return a>b? a:b; }
function and(a,b){ return a<b? a:b; }
function imp(a,b){ // a -> b is (!a) OR b
  return or(not(a), b);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to detect unsat under extra constraints? 
- How does the choice of truth ordering affect propagation?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests reasoning with three-valued logic in policy-like constraints, ensuring candidates can propagate values and avoid false evaluations when handling uncertainty.

## Key Concepts
- Three-valued logic with T/F/U truth values
- Implication defined as ¬X ∨ Y
- Constraint propagation and backtracking in constraint satisfaction problems
- Handling unknown values without forcing false evaluations

## Code Example
```javascript
function implies(X,Y){
  const not = {T:'F',F:'T',U:'U'};
  const max = (a,b)=> (a==='T'||b==='T')?'T':(a==='U'||b==='U')?'U':'F';
  return max(not[X], Y);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests design and reasoning about 3-valued constraint systems with cycles, using Kleene semantics and backtracking.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic: T>U>F; not maps T<->F, U->U; and/or as min/max
- CSP propagation with non-classical values and cycles
- Depth-limited backtracking to illustrate pruning

## Code Example

```javascript
// Kleene evaluator (ASCII semantics)
const rank = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
function not(x){ return x==='T'?'F': x==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function and(a,b){ return rank[a] < rank[b] ? a : b; }
function or(a,b){ return rank[a] > rank[b] ? a : b; }
function implies(a,b){ return or(not(a), b); }
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale to N predicates with arbitrary Horn-like rules while keeping backtracking practical?
- Compare 3-valued propagation vs. DPLL-style search with clause learning in this setting.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to apply public announcements to epistemic models and design a practical solver that scales with model size.

## Key Concepts
- Kripke models and accessibility relations
- Public announcements and product update
- Knowledge operator Ki and post-update evaluation
- Linear-time propagation and fixpoint reasoning

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal representation and product update sketch
function productUpdate(W, relations, V, pSet) {
  const Wp = W.filter(w => pSet.has(w));
  const Rp = {};
  for (const [AG, rel] of Object.entries(relations)) {
    Rp[AG] = new Map();
    for (const w of Wp) {
      Rp[AG].set(w, rel.get(w).filter(v => pSet.has(v)));
    }
  }
  return { W: Wp, relations: Rp };
}

function knows(pSet, w, agentRel) {
  const targets = agentRel.get(w) || [];
  return targets.every(u => pSet.has(u));
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How to extend to more agents or multi-modal knowledge (common knowledge)?
- How would you optimize for many sequential announcements or large models?
- What are potential numerical issues with non-deterministic updates in practice?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about constraints in practical feature-flag settings and to design a solver using propagation and backtracking rather than pure search. It also exposes how to craft a constructive counterexample when needed.

## Key Concepts

- Constraint satisfaction with implications and disjunctions
- Unit propagation, backtracking, and a decision trail
- Real-world constraints in deployment feature flags and release gates

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal backtracker for boolean flags with given constraints
function sat(S){
  return (!S.S1 || S.S2) && (!S.S2 || S.S3) && (!S.S4 || !S.S1) && (S.S1 || S.S4) && (S.S2 || S.S4);
}
function satPartial(part){
  const v = (k)=> part.hasOwnProperty(k)? part[k] : null;
  if (v('S1')===true && v('S2')===false) return false;
  if (v('S2')===true && v('S3')===false) return false;
  if (v('S4')===true && v('S1')===true) return false;
  if (v('S1')===false && v('S4')===false) return false;
  if (v('S2')===false && v('S4')===false) return false;
  return true;
}
function solve(partial={}, idx=0){
  const vars=['S1','S2','S3','S4'];
  if (!satPartial(partial)) return null;
  if (idx===vars.length) return partial;
  const name = vars[idx];
  for (const val of [false,true]){
    const next = Object.assign({}, partial, {[name]: val});
    const res = solve(next, idx+1);
    if (res) return res;
  }
  return null;
}
console.log(solve());
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this to thousands of flags with mostly Horn clauses?
- What optimizations would you add for large constraint sets?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests intuition for tri-valued semantics in graph-constraint problems, not just static formulas.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic behavior of implication; - graph-based propagation; - fixed-point iteration.

## Code Example
```javascript
function valid(edges, vals){
  for (const [a,b] of edges){
    const ta = vals[a], tb = vals[b];
    if (ta === 'T' && tb === 'F') return false;
  }
  return true;
}
```

```javascript
function propagate(vals, edges){
  let changed = true;
  while (changed){
    changed = false;
    for (const [a,b] of edges){
      if (vals[a] === 'T' and vals[b] === 'F'){ vals[b] = 'F'; changed = true; }
    }
  }
  return vals;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to scale to larger graphs with cycles? 
- How does adding probabilistic weights affect the approach?
